insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('telling-your-children-you-are-separating', 'Telling Your Children You Are Separating', 'The conversation most separating parents dread. How to plan it, what to say in the first thirty seconds, which three things to state outright, and what to leave out of it entirely.', 'You have decided, or it has been decided, and now there is a conversation to have with people who did not choose any of it. Most parents dread this more than any other part of separating.

You cannot make this painless. What you can do is make it clear, kind and free of things they have to carry for you, and that difference lasts for years.

Plan it before you say it

Agree the words with the other parent first, in writing if talking is hard. What you are saying, what is happening next, and what you are both not going to say.

Tell them together where that is possible, even if being in a room together is unpleasant for an hour. Hearing it from both of you at once says this is a shared decision and not a side to pick.

Pick a time with space after it. Not bedtime, not before school, not on the way out. A weekend morning with a free afternoon lets the questions come in waves, which is how they actually come.

Tell all the children at once if they are close in age, then follow up one to one. A teenager and a six-year-old need the same facts and completely different conversations.

Say the plain version first

Children need the headline before the explanation, and adults tend to do it the other way around.

Something like: we have decided to live in different homes. This is not going to change. We both love you and that will not change either.

Then stop and let it land. The silence is unbearable and it is theirs to fill.

Keep it short. Three or four sentences is the whole announcement. Everything else is answering what they ask.

Answer the questions they actually have

Their questions are rarely the ones you rehearsed. They are almost always logistics, because logistics is where a child''s safety lives.

Where will I sleep. Will I change schools. What about the dog. Will I still see Grandma. Can I still do swimming on Tuesdays. Who will take me.

Answer what you know plainly, and for what you do not know, say that: we have not worked that out yet, and when we do, we will tell you straight away. A child can hold not knowing. What they cannot hold is sensing that they are being managed.

Say the three things out loud

Children fill gaps with themselves, so say these explicitly rather than hoping they are obvious.

This is not your fault, and nothing you did or said caused it.

You cannot fix it, and it is not your job to try.

We both love you, and you are allowed to love us both.

That last one matters more than it sounds. Children will hide their affection for one parent to protect the other if they are not told plainly that they do not have to.

Leave the adult reasons out

The reasons are real and they are yours. A child who is handed them is handed a verdict to reach about a parent they love, and that is too heavy for anyone.

So no blame, no detail about money, nobody else''s name, no reading them messages, no asking what the other parent said. However unfair that feels, and sometimes it is very unfair, you are not giving up the truth. You are keeping a child out of a courtroom.

If someone has behaved badly, children usually work it out in their own time, as adults, with the whole picture. That version does them far less damage than yours.

Expect reactions that do not look like sadness

Some children cry. Many say nothing, go to their room, or ask if they can go and play, which parents often read as it did not sink in. It did.

Watch for anger at whoever is nearest, going quiet, going backward in sleep or behavior, suddenly becoming very good and helpful, or stomach aches and headaches with no cause.

All of that is normal in the early weeks. Keep the ordinary things running, because a familiar Tuesday does more for a frightened child than any conversation.

Then say it again. Once is an announcement. The same conversation, picked up casually over months, is how it gets believed.

Look after your own state

You will be grieving while being the steady one, which is the hardest ask in this. Children cope best when at least one adult near them is holding together, and holding together is not the same as being fine.

So have somewhere for your own feelings that is not your child, your child''s hearing, or the other parent. A friend, a sibling, a therapist, a support line. Crying in front of them sometimes is honest and fine. Being comforted by them regularly is not, because it quietly makes them the parent.

Tell the school. One email so a teacher knows why the homework slipped and can keep an eye out.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor or a family therapist if a child''s distress is not easing after a couple of months, if they are not sleeping or eating, if they are refusing school, or if their mood has dropped and stayed down. Family counseling is often short and practical, and a few sessions with a professional who works with separating families can take a great deal of weight off the way you talk to each other. For yourself, do not wait until you are at the end of it to ask for support. If you are having thoughts of ending your life, or of not being here, please tell your doctor or someone close to you today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Parenting', 'Loss']::text[], 0, '2026-10-29T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-your-appetite-disappears', 'When Your Appetite Disappears', 'Food has stopped appealing and you forget to eat. Why appetite goes when you are struggling, how to eat by the clock instead of by hunger, and the signs worth seeing a doctor about.', 'Food has stopped being interesting. You get to the middle of the afternoon and realize you have not eaten, and the thought of a plate of anything is faintly unpleasant rather than appealing.

People tend to treat this as a minor thing, or even a lucky one. It is not. Not eating makes everything else you are dealing with harder, and the reason it is happening usually deserves attention.

Why it happens

Appetite is one of the first things to go when you are struggling, and it goes by a few different routes.

Low mood flattens the signal. The same system that stops things being enjoyable stops food being appealing, and the hunger cue itself gets quiet.

Anxiety does the opposite thing to the same end. A body braced for something shuts digestion down, which is why worry comes with a closed throat and a stomach that says no.

Grief, exhaustion and pain all do it too. So do some medications, and so does simply having got out of the habit, because appetite is partly learned and it fades when nothing arrives at the usual hour.

Eat by the clock, not by the signal

This is the single most useful change. If the hunger cue is broken, waiting for it means waiting all day, so take the decision away from how you feel.

Set three or four times and eat something at each of them, whether or not you want to. Not a meal necessarily. Something.

Appetite tends to come back after eating rather than before it. The first few bites are the hard part, and the wanting often arrives halfway through, which is a strange and useful thing to know.

Make the portion small enough to be unthreatening

A full plate when nothing appeals is a reason to walk away, and the smaller version gets eaten.

Half of what you think you should have. A side plate rather than a dinner plate. Something you can finish, so that finishing is possible.

Then, if you want more, have more. Starting small is not giving up on eating properly. It is removing the obstacle.

Go for the easy things when chewing feels like work

On the days when food is a chore, the goal is not a balanced plate. It is getting something in.

Things that tend to go down when little else will: milk, yogurt, soup, cheese and crackers, peanut butter on toast, eggs, bananas, ice cream, a smoothie, a nutrition drink from the pharmacy.

Drinking is easier than eating for most people, so a glass of milk or a smoothie counts and counts well.

Cold and plain beats hot and strong-smelling. Smell drives a lot of early appetite, and a kitchen full of it can switch you off before you sit down.

Take the cooking out of it

Cooking is several decisions, a stretch of standing up, and a mess to clear. Any one of those can be the thing that stops you eating.

So keep food that needs nothing: bread, cereal, fruit, cheese, canned soup, frozen meals, nuts, the pre-cut things that cost more and are worth it this month.

Let somebody else cook if there is anyone to ask, and let the answer to how are you be I am finding it hard to eat. People can help with that.

Eat with someone if that helps, or alone if company makes it harder. Both are true for different people.

Watch for the things worth noticing

Most of this settles as whatever caused it settles. A few things mean it should be looked at sooner rather than later.

Weight dropping without trying, clothes getting loose, feeling faint or dizzy when you stand, being too tired to do ordinary things, hair thinning, feeling cold all the time.

Pain on swallowing, food sticking, throwing up, or a stomach that hurts after eating is a reason to see a doctor regardless of how your mood is, because that is a body question and not a mood one.

And if you notice relief at not eating, or a part of you that likes the number going down, that is worth saying out loud to someone. It is far more common than people admit and it is treatable.

Treat it as information

A missing appetite is usually your body reporting that something is too much. That report is worth reading rather than ignoring.

So ask what changed. A loss, a job, a diagnosis, a stretch of not sleeping, a new medication, a season of trying to hold everything up. The food will often follow once the thing underneath has somewhere to go.

In the meantime, eating by the clock keeps you functioning well enough to deal with it. People underestimate how much of the fog, the short fuse and the flat afternoons come from having eaten almost nothing.

When to get help

Please see your doctor if your appetite has been gone for more than a couple of weeks, if you are losing weight without meaning to, or if eating hurts, because both the mood reasons and the physical reasons are worth ruling in or out properly. Say plainly how little you are managing, and if a medication started around the same time, mention it, since a dose or a switch may be all it takes. A therapist can help with the low mood or anxiety underneath, and a dietitian is practical help rather than a lecture. If you are feeling hopeless, or you have had thoughts of not wanting to be here, please tell someone today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Food', 'Depression']::text[], 0, '2026-10-29T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('giving-someone-news-they-do-not-want', 'Giving Someone News They Do Not Want', 'A decision has gone against them, or a job is ending, and you have to say so. Why softening the words causes the damage, and how to be clear, steady and genuinely kind instead.', 'You have to tell somebody something they will not want to hear. A decision has gone against them, a job is ending, a plan is off, a diagnosis is what it is, an answer is no.

You have been rehearsing it for days, you have thought of nine ways to soften it, and all nine involve not quite saying it. That is the part worth fixing, because the softening is usually what causes the damage.

Say it in the first thirty seconds

The most common mistake is the long run-up. You start with context, you explain the process, you set out the reasons, and the person opposite you already knows and is now waiting in a state of dread while you talk.

So say it early and plainly. I have some difficult news. The position is being closed. Or: the scan showed something we need to talk about. Or: I have decided not to go ahead.

Then stop talking. The silence after is not a gap you need to fill. It is where they catch up.

Use clear words rather than kind-sounding ones

Soft language is not kindness. It forces someone to decode a sentence while in shock, and people routinely leave these conversations unsure what they were told.

Say ending, not transitioning. Say no, not probably not right now. Say died, not passed. If the answer is final, say that it is final, because a person who hears maybe will spend weeks on the maybe.

One clear sentence they can repeat to someone else that evening is the goal.

Do not borrow their reaction

You will want to apologize a great deal, explain how hard this was for you, or say you know exactly how they feel. All of that moves the conversation to you, and they then have to look after you.

One honest sentence is enough: I am sorry, and I wish I had better news. Then stay quiet and stay in the room.

If they get angry, let them. Anger in the first minute is almost never about you, even when it is pointed at you. Do not defend, do not match it, do not correct small inaccuracies. Just stay.

Let there be a reaction you did not expect. People laugh, go blank, ask about something trivial, or say thank you. None of that means it did not land.

Give the facts, then the next step

Once it has registered, people want two things: whether anything can change, and what happens now.

Answer the first honestly. If the decision is open to appeal, say how. If it is not, say so clearly rather than leaving a door ajar out of pity.

Then make the next step small and concrete. I will email you this today. We will meet again on Thursday. Here is who to call. Here is what happens to your pay.

Write it down and send it. Almost nobody remembers detail after the first sentence, and the reason they ask you the same question tomorrow is not that they were not listening.

Set it up so it can be survived

Where you do it changes how it goes.

In person if you can, by phone if you cannot, never by message for anything serious. Privately, with the door shut and your phone away. With time after it, so they are not turned out into a hallway thirty seconds later.

Think about what happens next for them. Who is outside. How they get home. Whether somebody should be with them. A conversation that ends with a person driving themselves home in shock is one you can plan better.

And give them a way to come back. Call me tomorrow if questions come up, and they will.

Handle your own part separately

Dreading this is not a character flaw. It is what it feels like to have power over an outcome somebody cares about, and the dread tends to produce exactly the behaviors that make it worse: delay, hints, over-explaining, and a promise you cannot keep.

So do not delay it to a better day, because there is not one, and the delay usually leaks. Do not hint beforehand to lessen the blow. Do not promise anything to make the moment easier.

Afterward, expect to feel bad even though you did it well. Tell someone, outside the situation and without the details, and give yourself the rest of the day at a lower setting.

If this is a regular part of your job, that accumulates. It is a real weight and it deserves acknowledging rather than absorbing.

When to get help

Please talk to a therapist if the dread of conversations like this is affecting your sleep or keeping you from doing your job, or if delivering bad news is a frequent part of your work and it has started to flatten you. If the news you are carrying is also your own loss, a diagnosis in the family or the end of something you wanted, you are doing two hard things at once, and talking to a professional about the second one is not an overreaction. Where you are supporting someone through what you told them, your doctor or a local service can point you both toward practical help rather than leaving it all with you.', array['Communication', 'Anxiety']::text[], 0, '2026-10-29T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('being-the-sober-one-at-the-party', 'Being the Sober One at the Party', 'One short sentence, repeated. Why holding a glass matters, how to handle the people who push, and why the evening gets harder after eleven whatever you are drinking.', 'You are not drinking tonight. Maybe you have stopped for good, maybe you are driving, maybe it is medication, maybe you just do not want to. And somehow this ordinary fact has become the most conspicuous thing about you in the room.

Within ten minutes somebody will notice, and you will discover how much explaining other people think a soft drink requires.

Decide your line before you arrive

The scramble happens because you are thinking about it for the first time with a glass being held out at you. So decide in the quiet beforehand.

Pick one short sentence and keep it the same all night. I am not drinking tonight. I am on something that does not mix. I am driving. I am good, thanks.

Short is what makes it work. A reason invites a conversation about the reason. A flat sentence with nothing after it ends it.

You are allowed to say nothing true at all, by the way. Your health is yours and a party is not the place you owe anyone an account of it.

Hold something

This sounds too simple to matter and it is one of the most effective things available.

An empty hand reads as waiting for a drink, and people fill it. A glass with something in it reads as sorted, and people move on.

So get something at the door and keep it topped up. Soda and lime, tonic, a cola, a non-alcoholic beer if that does not make it harder for you, anything in a proper glass rather than a plastic cup.

If you are at a bar, order first and order confidently. Hesitation is what gets questioned.

Handle the pushers without a debate

Most people do not care. A few will push, and it is almost never about you. It is about them wanting company, or being unsettled by someone not drinking, or already being past the point of tact.

Repeat your line word for word rather than finding a new answer. Honestly, I am good. Honestly, I am good. Repetition is boring and boring is what you want.

Change the subject in the same breath. How is the new place. Did you end up going. People follow the new question gratefully.

Do not get drawn into how long it has been, or why, or whether just the one would hurt. Not tonight, not standing up, not with an audience. I would rather not get into it here is a complete sentence.

Have a job

Being the sober one at a loose event is unstructured, and unstructured is when you start counting the minutes.

So give yourself something: take the photos, mind the music, keep the food going, drive people home, talk to the person standing on their own at the edge, who is usually glad of you.

Having a role means you are doing something rather than noticing that you are not drinking, and most of the awkwardness lives in that noticing.

Know that the evening has two halves

The first half is usually fine. Then it tips. Conversations get louder and more repetitive, and the connection you were enjoying at nine o''clock is not available at eleven.

That is not you going flat. It is the room changing. Plan for it and you will stop reading it as a failure.

So go early, when the talking is good. Decide your leaving time before you arrive, and leave at it without an announcement or an apology. Arrange your own way home so that staying is never the easier option.

Saying good night is the one thing people do not question.

Expect your own discomfort and let it be there

Even with every line prepared, the first few events are uncomfortable, and that is not a sign you are doing it wrong.

You may feel on the outside of something, or boring, or that you are being watched. Some of that is accurate: you are doing something unusual in a room built around one thing. Most of it fades after a handful of occasions, as it stops being new to you and to everyone else.

It helps to notice what you get. Remembering the conversation. Driving home. Waking up clear. No morning of combing back through what you said.

And it helps to have one person in the room who knows. Not an announcement, just somebody you can stand next to who will not offer you a drink.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if you are stopping after drinking heavily or daily, because coming off alcohol can need medical support and stopping suddenly on your own is not always safe. If you are not drinking because drinking had become a problem, a therapist or a local alcohol service will give you more than willpower will, and a group of people doing the same thing takes much of the loneliness out of it. If social events are becoming something you avoid altogether, or the anxiety beforehand is affecting your week, that is worth taking to a professional in its own right.', array['Assertiveness', 'Social Anxiety']::text[], 0, '2026-10-29T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('working-nights-and-sleeping-days', 'Working Nights and Sleeping Days', 'Night work is not daytime shifted along. How to protect the sleep you can get, when to use light and caffeine, the drive home that is the real danger, and the quieter costs.', 'You finish at seven in the morning and the world is starting its day as you go to bed. The light is wrong, the street is loud, your phone is busy, and your body has an opinion about all of it.

Night work is not simply daytime shifted along. You are asking your body to be awake when it is set up for sleep and to sleep when it is set up for alertness, and that mismatch is most of what makes it hard.

Protect the sleep you can get

The sleep is the part you can actually change, so it gets the most effort.

Darkness is the main lever. Blackout curtains, a shade, and then a sleep mask on top, because gaps at the edges are what wake you. Light reaching your eyes in the morning is the strongest signal your body has for stay awake, and blocking it is more effective than anything else on this list.

Noise next. Earplugs, or steady sound from a fan or a machine, which works better than silence because it covers the sudden noises rather than leaving them exposed.

Keep the room cool, put the phone out of reach, and tell the people you live with the hours. A note on the door helps more than you would think, and so does the household knowing that eleven in the morning is your three in the morning.

Keep the same schedule on days off if you can bear it, or shift it only a few hours. Flipping back and forth every week is the version that leaves people worst off, though family life sometimes makes that unavoidable and a compromise is better than nothing.

Split it if one block will not hold

Many night workers cannot sleep seven hours straight after a shift, and fighting for that can be wasted effort.

Four or five hours in the morning and a nap before you go in often works better, and the pre-shift nap is worth protecting even when it is short. Twenty minutes helps. Ninety is better if it fits.

Do not lie there for hours. If sleep is not coming after twenty or thirty minutes, get up, do something dull in low light, and go back when you feel heavy.

Use light and caffeine deliberately

These are your two strongest tools and timing is everything with both.

Bright light during the shift, especially in the first half, helps you stay alert. Dim things down toward the end so you are not getting a jolt of wake-up right before bed.

Wear sunglasses on the way home, even in winter. That short walk or drive in morning light is often what makes the sleep that follows so poor.

Caffeine early in the shift rather than late. It stays in the body for hours, and the cup at five in the morning is why you are staring at the ceiling at eight. Alcohol is the other one to watch. It puts you under and then breaks the second half of your sleep.

Eat like it is a working day

Your digestion is slower at night, so heavy food at three in the morning tends to sit badly.

A proper meal before the shift, something light in the middle of it, and a small breakfast rather than a large one before bed. Keep food out of the last hour before sleeping.

Water matters more than people expect, and so does moving. A short walk on a break does more for the middle-of-the-night slump than another coffee.

Watch the safety edges

The hours after a night shift are the risky ones, and tiredness is poor at assessing itself.

Driving home is the single biggest danger. If the drive is long, nap before it, share the ride, or take public transportation. Know the warning signs, which are missing a turn, drifting, and no memory of the last few minutes.

Be careful with anything that needs judgment in the last hours of a shift, and double-check rather than trusting that you feel fine.

Mind the quieter costs

Nights take a social toll that rarely gets counted. You miss evenings, you are asleep for the weekend, and the people around you are on a different clock, which is lonelier than the tiredness.

So put the things that matter on the calendar deliberately rather than hoping for gaps. One regular evening or meal a week that everyone knows is yours is worth a lot.

And watch your mood over months rather than days. Long stretches of night work leave many people flatter than they realize, and that is a reason to take it seriously, not to push through.

When to get help

Please see your doctor if you cannot sleep even with the room dark and quiet, if you are falling asleep when you should not be, if you snore heavily or wake gasping, or if your mood has been low for weeks, because sleep disorders are common in shift workers and treatable. Say plainly what your hours are, since advice built for a day schedule will not fit. Ask your employer about the schedule as well, because some patterns are far harder on a body than others and that is a legitimate conversation to have. A therapist can help if the sleeplessness has turned into dread of going to bed, which is its own problem on top of the hours.', array['Sleep', 'Stress']::text[], 0, '2026-10-29T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
  excerpt      = excluded.excerpt,
  body_md      = excluded.body_md,
  tags         = excluded.tags,
  min_level    = 0,
  published_at = excluded.published_at;
